course: dictionary
lesson: 11738
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can work as more than one part of speech. Babysitting can be a verb or a noun. The words around it give you clues. «Mum is babysitting her little brother tonight.» Here babysitting is a verb, because it tells you what Mum is doing.
explain: «Babysitting pays well if you do it every Saturday.» Here babysitting is a noun: it names the job itself. Ask yourself: is someone doing an action, or is this the name of a thing or an activity? Try swapping in "looking after children" and check the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Maths problems use this word too. «Ava earns 5 pounds an hour for babysitting.» If she works 4 hours, then 4 × 5 = 20, so she earns 20 pounds. The word tells you what the money is for, and the numbers tell you how much.
fact: Babysitting means looking after a child while their parents are out.
fact: As a verb, babysitting is the -ing form of babysit.
fact: As a noun, babysitting is the job of looking after children, often for pay.
fact: Babysitting is uncountable, so you do not add a or an to it: you say «some babysitting», not «a babysitting».
fact: Babysitting courses teach first aid and what to do in an emergency.
exercise: «Dad is babysitting his nephew while Mum works late.» What part of speech is babysitting here? | Noun | *Verb | Adverb | Adjective
exercise: «Her babysitting paid for a new bike.» What does babysitting mean in this sentence? | *The job of looking after children | Cooking a meal for the family | Playing games outside | Going to bed early
exercise: Ella earns 5 pounds an hour babysitting. She works 4 hours. How much does she earn? | 1 pound | *20 pounds | 9 pounds | 45 pounds
test: «Ravi is babysitting the twins on Friday night.» What does babysitting mean here? | Cooking dinner for the family | Reading a book aloud | Playing football with friends | *Looking after the twins while their parents are out
test: Why might a babysitting course teach first aid? | *So you know what to do in an emergency | So you can cook faster | To learn how to draw | To help you win a sports prize
exam: «Babysitting is hard work, but Maya loves it.» What word class is babysitting here? | A verb that describes an action happening now | An adjective describing Maya | A place where children play | *A noun, the job of looking after children
